= Background and Related Work <sec-related>

*DESMET.* The DESMET methodology provides a structured basis for evaluating software engineering methods and tools when controlled experiments are impractical @kitchenham1997desmet @kitchenham1998desmet_eval. It separates quantitative evaluation (measurable effects), qualitative evaluation (feature analysis against explicit criteria), and hybrids, and among its nine methods two apply directly here: _qualitative screening_ and _benchmarking_. DESMET's guidance on scale granularity motivates the 0--3 rubric: coarser scales are more reliable when a single evaluator cannot cross-validate. Ferrari et~al. applied DESMET to formal-methods tools for railway signalling, combining benchmarking with usability assessment @ferrari2021systematic; the present work adopts the same hybrid stance for a different tool class.

*Comparisons of agentic platforms.* Broccia et~al. compare eight GenAI workflow platforms on system-level features (MCP support, local versus remote LLMs, SDK dependence, monitoring) and interaction-level features (no-code versus low-code, collaboration, human-in-the-loop) @broccia2025humainflow; the comparison is confined to visual platforms and to what the platforms _offer_, not how they perform. Derouiche et~al. compare six agentic frameworks architecturally---communication, memory, orchestration, guardrails---without benchmarking @derouiche2025agentic. Yin et~al. provide the closest empirical precedent, evaluating seven agent frameworks on three code-centric tasks across effectiveness, efficiency, and overhead @yin2025comprehensive, and show that no single framework dominates; they report task success but do not instrument intra-stage orchestration. Adimulam et~al. argue that value in multi-agent systems arises from orchestrated interaction rather than individual capability @adimulam2026orchestration, and the BAMAS framework of Yang et~al. treats orchestration topology as a budgeted design variable @yang2025bamas; both support measuring the framework as a first-class object. MultiAgentBench benchmarks collaboration across star, chain, tree, and graph topologies with milestone-based indicators @zhu2025multiagentbench, and Mohammadi et~al. survey what should be evaluated in LLM agents and how @mohammadi2025evaluation; neither isolates the platform from the model. Shu et~al. propose assertion-based benchmarking that avoids ground-truth trajectories @shu2024towards.

*Benchmarks and harnesses.* SWE-bench @jimenez2024swebench, HumanEval @chen2021humaneval, MBPP @austin2021mbpp, and AgentBench @liu2023agentbench measure what a model, or an agent built around one, can produce on fixed tasks; they do not separate the surrounding framework's contribution. On the execution side, the lm-evaluation-harness @gao2024lmevalharness standardises how few-shot tasks are run and reported, HELM @liang2023helm argues for a matrix of metrics over a single accuracy score, and CLEAR @mehta2025clear carries that multi-dimensional view into enterprise agents; all stop at the model boundary. Surveys of LLM agents for software engineering @dong2025survey @wang2024survey note that platform-level comparison remains unaddressed.

*Gap.* No prior work drives architecturally heterogeneous agentic platforms through a multi-stage software engineering pipeline under one adapter contract and reports framework-centric metrics with the model held constant. That is the question the harness is built to answer.
